# =============================================================================
# nest-server-starter — Environment Variables
# =============================================================================
# Copy to `.env` and adjust values.
#
# Legend:
#   - Uncommented lines → REQUIRED for a deployed environment (`develop`,
#     `test`, `production`). Missing values fail the server startup.
#   - Commented lines  → OPTIONAL (framework default applies).
#
# Local-only environments (`local`, `ci`, `e2e`) ship public dummy secrets
# and Mailhog defaults in `src/config.env.ts` — they run completely without
# a `.env` file.
#
# Two ways to configure (both supported):
#   1. NSC__-prefixed env vars → auto-merged into the config tree by
#      `getEnvironmentConfig`. Path: `NSC__FOO__BAR` → `config.foo.bar`,
#      single underscore = camelCase boundary.
#      (e.g. `NSC__BETTER_AUTH__SECRET` → `config.betterAuth.secret`)
#   2. Direct env vars (e.g. `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`):
#      read explicitly in `src/config.env.ts` for values that need parsing.


# =============================================================================
# Database (required in deployed envs)
# =============================================================================

NSC__MONGOOSE__URI=mongodb://localhost:27017/nest-server-starter-production


# =============================================================================
# URLs (required in deployed envs)
# =============================================================================

# API base URL — used for CORS, Passkey RP ID, email links, OAuth callbacks.
# The frontend/app URL is auto-derived: api.example.com → example.com
NSC__BASE_URL=https://api.example.com

# Frontend/app URL (optional — auto-derived from BASE_URL if unset)
# NSC__APP_URL=https://example.com


# =============================================================================
# Authentication Secrets (required in deployed envs)
# =============================================================================

# Better-Auth secret — signs session cookies + BetterAuth JWTs. Minimum 32 characters.
# Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
NSC__BETTER_AUTH__SECRET=CHANGE_ME_generate_32plus_chars_with_node_crypto_randomBytes

# Legacy JWT secrets — ONLY required if a project opts back into Legacy Auth
# endpoints by setting `auth.legacyEndpoints.enabled: true` in src/config.env.ts.
# The starter ships BetterAuth-only; leave unset for new projects.
# Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(64).toString('base64'))"
# NSC__JWT__SECRET=CHANGE_ME_generate_64plus_chars_with_node_crypto_randomBytes
# NSC__JWT__REFRESH__SECRET=CHANGE_ME_different_64plus_chars_than_JWT_SECRET

# Cookie prefix (optional — only for multiple apps on a SHARED parent domain).
# The Better-Auth session cookie is named `<prefix>.session_token`; the prefix
# defaults to the IAM base path (`iam` → `iam.session_token`). Override it ONLY
# when several apps share one parent domain with cross-subdomain cookies (e.g.
# `api.example.com` + `app2.example.com` both deriving cookie domain
# `.example.com`) — without distinct prefixes their cookies collide and break
# sign-in. Subdomain-isolated setups (`api.crm.localhost` vs `api.shop.localhost`,
# i.e. the default `lt dev` layout) do NOT need this. If set, the frontend MUST
# use the SAME value via `NUXT_PUBLIC_COOKIE_PREFIX`. Allowed chars: [A-Za-z0-9._-].
# Read directly from the environment by @lenne.tech/nest-server (no config.env.ts wiring).
# COOKIE_PREFIX=myapp


# =============================================================================
# Email — SMTP required in deployed envs, Brevo optional overlay
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP (required — baseline transport for ALL outgoing mail)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Any SMTP provider: AWS SES, SendGrid, Postmark, Mailgun, self-hosted Postfix.
# Handles all auth flows and custom app emails.
NSC__EMAIL__DEFAULT_SENDER__EMAIL=noreply@example.com
NSC__EMAIL__SMTP__HOST=smtp.example.com
NSC__EMAIL__SMTP__AUTH__USER=smtp-user
NSC__EMAIL__SMTP__AUTH__PASS=smtp-password
# NSC__EMAIL__DEFAULT_SENDER__NAME=Nest Server Starter   # default: project brand name
# SMTP_PORT=587                                          # default: 587
# SMTP_SECURE=true                                       # default: derived from the port — true on :465, STARTTLS otherwise.
#                                                        # Only set this for a host that contradicts that convention.
# SMTP_REQUIRE_TLS=false                                 # default: true — refuse to send if the server offers no STARTTLS.
#                                                        # Set "false" only for a host that cannot upgrade; the session is then plaintext.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Brevo Transactional API (optional overlay for template-based mails)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Brevo (ex-Sendinblue) sends via HTTPS with server-side templates.
# Runs IN PARALLEL to SMTP — only used when a module references a
# `brevoTemplateId` (e.g. `betterAuth.emailVerification.brevoTemplateId`).
# All other mails still flow through the SMTP transport above.
# Get an API key at https://app.brevo.com/settings/keys/api
# BREVO_API_KEY=xkeysib-your-brevo-api-key
#
# Request limits (since nest-server 11.32.0). The Brevo SDK defaults to 2 retries
# honouring Retry-After with a 60s cap PER attempt and no timeout — and sends are
# awaited inside request handlers, so a rate-limited Brevo could park a user-facing
# request for ~2 minutes. Defaults below keep the request fast.
# BREVO_MAX_RETRIES=0
# BREVO_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# BREVO_THROW_ON_ERROR=true    # make a failed send throw instead of resolving to null


# =============================================================================
# CORS (optional — origins auto-derived from BASE_URL + APP_URL)
# =============================================================================

# Additional allowed origins (comma-separated, merged with APP_URL + BASE_URL).
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://admin.example.com,https://partner.example.com


# =============================================================================
# Feature Toggles (optional — sensible defaults apply)
# =============================================================================

# Two-Factor Authentication app name — shown in Authenticator apps (TOTP issuer).
# Default: project brand name from src/config.env.ts.
# TWO_FACTOR_APP_NAME=My App


# =============================================================================
# Social Login (optional — BetterAuth OAuth providers)
# =============================================================================
# Set both ID and SECRET to activate a provider.

# NSC__BETTER_AUTH__SOCIAL_PROVIDERS__GOOGLE__CLIENT_ID=
# NSC__BETTER_AUTH__SOCIAL_PROVIDERS__GOOGLE__CLIENT_SECRET=
# NSC__BETTER_AUTH__SOCIAL_PROVIDERS__GITHUB__CLIENT_ID=
# NSC__BETTER_AUTH__SOCIAL_PROVIDERS__GITHUB__CLIENT_SECRET=


# =============================================================================
# Initial Admin (optional — first deployment only; remove after setup)
# =============================================================================
# Creates a single admin user on first startup if the user collection is empty.

# NSC__SYSTEM_SETUP__INITIAL_ADMIN__EMAIL=admin@example.com
# NSC__SYSTEM_SETUP__INITIAL_ADMIN__PASSWORD=YourSecurePassword123!
# NSC__SYSTEM_SETUP__INITIAL_ADMIN__NAME=Admin


# =============================================================================
# Migrations (optional — nest-server 11.31.1+)
# =============================================================================
# Strict integrity mode for `migrate up` (run by docker-entrypoint.sh on boot):
# fail startup when a migration recorded in the state store has no file on disk,
# instead of the default (tolerate with a warning). Recommended `true` for
# immutable production images — a missing migration file there can only mean a
# broken build or a wrong database. Accepts 1|true|yes (case-insensitive).

# NSC__MIGRATE__STRICT=true


# =============================================================================
# Reverse proxy (optional — but REQUIRED as soon as you rate-limit; 11.33.0+)
# =============================================================================
# Express `trust proxy`, passed through by CoreModule. Both auth rate limiters
# key their counter on `request.ip`. With nothing configured Express reports the
# SOCKET peer — which behind Caddy/nginx/Traefik/a Kubernetes ingress/a cloud LB
# is the PROXY, identically for every request. All your clients then share ONE
# bucket and the limit throttles all of them at once. A boot warning names this
# when a limiter is on and the value is unset.
#
# The value is the number of proxies that actually sit in front of THIS
# deployment — no template can know it, which is why it ships unset.
#   1      exactly one reverse proxy in front of the app (the usual case)
#   2      a reverse proxy behind a CDN
#   false  nothing proxies us — states it explicitly and silences the warning
# NEVER `true` on a public deployment: it trusts whatever the client sent, which
# hands every caller its own bucket and removes the protection entirely.
# Count the hops: trusting more than really sit there is the bypass in reverse.

# NSC__TRUST_PROXY=1


# Rate limit for all /iam/* endpoints, on the IP axis. OFF unless you set the max.
#
# Since nest-server 11.36.1 `POST /iam/request-password-reset` is live, unauthenticated,
# token-minting and mail-sending. The mailer's per-recipient cooldown (60 s) is always on
# and covers mail-bombing one address; this bounds the other axis — address enumeration
# and mail-cost amplification across many addresses from rotating IPs.
#
# Set NSC__TRUST_PROXY too when running behind a reverse proxy, or every client shares
# one bucket and the limit protects nothing.

# IAM_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10
# IAM_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60


# =============================================================================
# Graceful shutdown (optional — nest-server 11.33.0+)
# =============================================================================
# Milliseconds to keep the instance FULLY HEALTHY after SIGTERM/SIGINT, before
# the app is closed. On a rolling deploy the orchestrator signals and
# deregisters at the same moment, but deregistration is not instant — this is
# the window in which those last in-flight requests still land on a healthy
# process. Default 0 (no delay, no log). Wired via `installGracefulShutdown()`
# in src/main.ts, which REPLACES `enableShutdownHooks()`.
#
# Keep it well below the orchestrator's grace period AND leave room for the
# drain that follows: Compose `stop_grace_period` defaults to 10s, Kubernetes
# `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` to 30s, and the framework's process
# diagnostics force-exit after 30s. Exceed any of them and the process is
# SIGKILLed mid-wait, running no shutdown hook at all. Values above 10000 log a
# warning; above 60000 the delay is capped.

# NSC__SHUTDOWN_DELAY_MS=5000


# =============================================================================
# Central Redis (optional — only needed for MORE THAN ONE replica; 11.33.0+)
# =============================================================================
# Absent = every feature below keeps its process-local behaviour, which is
# exactly right for a single instance. Presence implies enabled, so merely
# setting one of these switches ALL of them on at once:
#   - auth + AI rate limits    counter per replica (max × replicas) → exact `max`
#   - cron jobs                every replica runs every tick        → deduplicated
#   - GraphQL subscriptions    publishing replica only              → cluster-wide
#   - tenant membership cache  local invalidation                   → broadcast
#   - Hub collectors           this pod's slice                     → merged view
#
# REQUIRES the optional peer `ioredis` (`pnpm add ioredis`) — configured but not
# installed FAILS THE BOOT with a named error. Add `bullmq` too for BullMQ-based
# cron schedulers (without it, cron falls back to per-tick Redis leases).
#
# Two knobs matter more than they look:
#   db         numbered Redis database. Sharing one Redis between environments
#              without separating them is how staging flushes production's
#              rate-limit counters.
#   keyPrefix  namespaces every key. Defaults to the project slug, so distinct
#              projects are separated already — two STAGES of the SAME project
#              are not, unless you set this (or `db`).
#
# GraphQL subscription payloads must be JSON-serialisable once Redis is in play:
# `Date`, class instances, `Map`/`Set` and `undefined` do not survive the trip.

# NSC__REDIS__HOST=redis
# NSC__REDIS__PORT=6379
# NSC__REDIS__DB=0
# NSC__REDIS__KEY_PREFIX=nest-server-starter-production
# NSC__REDIS__USERNAME=
# NSC__REDIS__PASSWORD=
# NSC__REDIS__URL=redis://user:pass@redis:6379/0   # takes precedence over host/port/db


# =============================================================================
# File storage (optional — default derives to GridFS; 11.33.0+)
# =============================================================================
# Where the file BYTES live: `gridfs` (default here), `s3`, or `filesystem`.
# Metadata always stays in MongoDB whichever driver holds the bytes — it has to
# remain queryable for `findFileInfo()` and for the ownership rule in
# `FileService.checkRights()`.
#
# Left unset the driver is DERIVED, most capable first: `s3` when `s3.bucket` is
# configured, else `gridfs` when a database is, else `filesystem`. Note what
# that means: setting NSC__S3__BUCKET alone MOVES YOUR FILES TO S3. If the S3
# block is meant for something else (own client, TUS staging only), pin the
# driver with NSC__FILE__STORAGE=gridfs.
#
# Set explicitly it is ENFORCED: an unavailable store FAILS THE BOOT rather than
# falling back. A silent fallback would put files in a store the operator does
# not believe they are in, with no way afterwards to tell which file went where.
#
# Switching is forward-only — reads consult ALL stores, so files written under a
# previous driver stay readable. No migration step, no cut-over.
#
# `filesystem` is POD-LOCAL: not shared between replicas, lost on restart unless
# `storageDir` is a mounted volume. Right for one instance or a real volume,
# wrong for a scaled service.
#
# BACKUPS: with `s3` or `filesystem` the metadata lives in the new collections
# `s3-files` / `filesystem-files`. Losing them orphans the bytes, because
# nothing else records what a file is called. Include them.

# NSC__FILE__STORAGE=gridfs
# NSC__FILE__STORAGE_DIR=uploads/files          # `filesystem` driver only

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# S3 (optional — REQUIRES the peer `@aws-sdk/client-s3`)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Configured but not installed FAILS THE BOOT with a named error. Presigned
# downloads additionally need `@aws-sdk/s3-request-presigner`, TUS S3 staging
# needs `@tus/s3-store`:
#   pnpm add @aws-sdk/client-s3 @aws-sdk/s3-request-presigner @tus/s3-store
#
# `bucket` is the one thing S3 cannot default — a block without it is ignored
# with a warning. Omit the credentials to use the AWS default credential chain
# (IAM role, instance profile, shared config).

# NSC__S3__BUCKET=my-app-files
# NSC__S3__REGION=eu-central-1
# NSC__S3__ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# NSC__S3__SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# NSC__S3__ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000           # self-hosted MinIO / RustFS
# NSC__S3__FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true                # required by most self-hosted stores
# NSC__S3__AUTO_CREATE_BUCKET=false             # default false: managed creds usually
#                                               # carry no CreateBucket, and creating a
#                                               # bucket because of a typo is worse than
#                                               # a clear error. Turn on for MinIO/RustFS.
# NSC__S3__ENABLED=false                        # park the config, inert, for later
#
# Presigned downloads make `GET /files/id/:id` answer 302 to a time-limited S3
# URL instead of streaming, taking the bytes off the API. The URL is a BEARER
# CAPABILITY: anyone holding it can fetch the object until it expires, with no
# session. Authorization happens once, at issue time — keep the expiry short and
# do not enable it for files whose audience is narrower than "anyone with the link".
# NSC__S3__PRESIGNED_DOWNLOADS__EXPIRES_IN_SECONDS=300
#
# TUS staging bucket for in-progress resumable uploads. Defaults to `bucket`,
# and `tus.s3Staging` is ON by default whenever S3 is usable — so a pre-existing
# S3 block also moves TUS staging off local disk. GIVE THE STAGING BUCKET A
# LIFECYCLE RULE THAT EXPIRES INCOMPLETE MULTIPART UPLOADS: the framework's own
# expiration cleanup is skipped in S3 mode, so aborted uploads otherwise
# accumulate parts that nothing removes.
# NSC__S3__STAGING_BUCKET=my-app-tus
# NSC__TUS__S3_STAGING=false                    # force TUS staging back to local disk


# =============================================================================
# Input whitelisting — security.mapAndValidatePipe
# =============================================================================
# Input properties WITHOUT an @UnifiedField decorator are SILENTLY STRIPPED by
# default. No error, no warning: a sign-up carrying firstName and lastName
# arrives at the service with only email and password. This is the single most
# surprising default in the framework and it is exactly what a port from an
# older codebase runs into — the DTO looks right, the request looks right, and
# the data is simply gone by the time you can log it.
#
# 'strip' (default) — drop unknown properties silently
# 'error'           — reject the request with LTNS_0303 instead. Turn this on
#                     while porting or debugging: it converts a silent data loss
#                     into a loud, located failure.
# false             — no whitelisting at all (not recommended)
# NSC__SECURITY__MAP_AND_VALIDATE_PIPE__NON_WHITELISTED_FIELDS=error


# =============================================================================
# Multi-tenancy (optional) — presence implies enabled
# =============================================================================
# Turning this on changes what EVERY query returns: the Mongoose tenant plugin
# scopes reads and writes to the tenant resolved from the request. Do not enable
# it on an existing single-tenant database without a migration plan.
#
# NSC__MULTI_TENANCY__HEADER_NAME=x-tenant-id      # default 'x-tenant-id'
# NSC__MULTI_TENANCY__MEMBERSHIP_MODEL=TenantMember
# NSC__MULTI_TENANCY__ADMIN_BYPASS=true            # default true
# NSC__MULTI_TENANCY__CACHE_TTL_MS=30000           # default 30000; 0 disables
#
# CAUTION: a membership role never confers global authority. Do NOT name a
# tenant role `admin` — the boot fails on that, deliberately, because the access
# decision would otherwise be ambiguous.


# =============================================================================
# TUS resumable uploads — the role default changed in 11.33
# =============================================================================
# `tus.roles` defaults to ['s_user'], NOT S_EVERYONE. A TUS upload writes into
# the same store the download routes guard, and the termination extension can
# delete from it — anonymous writes into a store only privileged callers may
# read is the wrong way round.
#
# Consequence: anonymous uploads that worked before 11.33 now return 401. If you
# accept attachments on a PUBLIC form, opt back in EXPLICITLY. Note that the
# CORS preflight (OPTIONS) is exempt either way, so a browser upload is not
# broken before the first byte.
# NEST_SERVER_CONFIG='{"tus":{"roles":["s_everyone"]}}'


# =============================================================================
# AI assistant (optional) — presence implies enabled
# =============================================================================
# The tool registry is EMPTY by default: the assistant has no access to any
# domain model until you register tools for it. Without them it correctly
# answers "I don't have a tool to do that" to every domain question.
# See src/core/modules/ai/INTEGRATION-CHECKLIST.md.
#
# NSC__AI__ENCRYPTION_SECRET=change-me-32-bytes-minimum
# NSC__AI__MAX_ITERATIONS=5                        # default 5
# NSC__AI__DEFAULT_MODE=auto                       # 'auto' | 'plan'
# NSC__AI__RATE_LIMIT__MAX=20                      # default 20
# NSC__AI__RATE_LIMIT__WINDOW_SECONDS=60           # default 60
#
# SSRF egress allowlist (nest-server 11.40.0+). Comma-separated hostnames an AI
# connection may call. UNSET = no restriction: any base URL an operator configures
# is dialled, which is the point to weigh before leaving this commented out.
# Entries are trimmed and lower-cased. A bare hostname allows the host on ANY port
# (`api.openai.com` covers `https://api.openai.com/v1` and `:9200` alike); adding a
# port narrows it to exactly that port (`llm.internal:8080` rejects `:9200`). Mind the
# scheme when you do: `:443` is the https default, so it does not match an `http://`
# URL.
# Before 11.40.0 a value set HERE was silently ignored — `NSC__*` variables arrive as
# strings and the check only accepted an array, so it returned early and the allowlist
# was inert. 11.40.0 parses the string and enforces it, which is why setting this can
# now block calls that used to go through.
# NSC__AI__ALLOWED_BASE_URL_HOSTS=api.openai.com,api.anthropic.com
